test(contract): decide observe_session's eleven MCP-006 cells, and retire the deferred verdict (#207)

Six reachable, five not, and the split is the finding. observe_session is the
only tool on the surface that is a COMPOSITION — a start resolves the workspace
through describe_workspace and mints the run through register_agent, a stop ends
it through retire_agent — so its error surface is very nearly theirs, inherited
unchanged because IP §4's vocabulary is closed and a second wording for one
failure sends a shim author to the wrong file.

That pulls two ways. Four classes that would be absurd for a tool "about
sessions" are reachable here for exactly register_agent's reasons, and two of
them are worth reading twice: RUN_NOT_FOUND is NOT a stop for a session that
never started — that is required to succeed with the terminal state, so the
obvious reading of the class is a bug here rather than a case — and
RUN_ALREADY_RETIRED is not a second stop either. Both survive from
register_agent's SPIRE paths: a missing attested parent, and an entry SPIRE
reports and then does not hold.

And the stop path produces no class at all, which is what a stop never blocking
means once it is written down as a matrix row. Every failure there is a
successful reply carrying the class in `detail`, so nothing a stop meets appears
on this side of the matrix. LEDGER_UNAVAILABLE is therefore driven through the
marker volume — this tool's own second store, and the half of the class only it
and observe_tool_call have — rather than through Postgres, which reaches the
same class one gate later inside register_agent where the dead-ledger group
already pins it.

DUPLICATE_REQUEST is reachable in exactly one way and it is not a duplicate
start: that returns the same run, which is the whole point of the derived key.
It is a mapping lost while the ledger kept the registration — a container
recreated without its marker volume — and the session then describing itself
differently under a key that is necessarily the same.

The battery gains fourteen rows and the loop loses its skip. Three of the rows
must SUCCEED, and a guard fails the test if none does: nothing configures this
tool in the entry point (#211), so an unconfigured one answers
INVARIANT_VIOLATION to every input — a class the matrix calls reachable — and
all fourteen rows would otherwise pass while proving nothing about the tool.
That is the vacuous shape this whole file is written against, and it is sharper
here than anywhere else on the surface.

The `deferred` verdict is removed with its case. RM-071 (#94) removed it once on
the reasoning that all five tools were bound; RM-131 (#210) made that premise
false and put it back with a guard; #207 makes it true again. Both paragraphs
are kept on the type rather than deleted, and what replaces the branch is the
positive assertion it was really protecting: every name on IP §4's surface must
HAVE a binder, so a ninth added ahead of its implementation fails there.

